<section class="box-exercise">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/24:_Amines_and_Heterocycles/24.09:_Heterocyclic_Amines" class="mt-section"><span id="Exercise_.5C(.5CPageIndex.7B2.7D.5C)"></span><h5 class="box-legend editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Exercise \(\PageIndex{2}\)</span></h5>

<p>Would you expect a thiazole ring to be protonated at the physiological pH of 7.3 <strong>(Section 24-5)</strong>?</p>

<dl>
    <dt><strong class="emphasis bold">Answer</strong></dt>
    <dd>
    <p>7.3 = 2.44 + log ([RNH<sub>2</sub>] / [RNH<sub>3</sub><sup>+</sup>])</p>

    <p>([RNH<sub>2</sub>] / [RNH<sub>3</sub><sup>+</sup>]) = 7.2 x 10<sup>4</sup></p>

    <p>. . . so, [RNH<sub>2</sub>] &gt;&gt; [RNH<sub>3</sub><sup>+</sup>] so thiazole would be almost completely unprotonated at pH = 7.3.</p>
    </dd>
</dl>
