</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/24:_Amines_and_Heterocycles/24.09:_Heterocyclic_Amines" class="mt-section" id="section_6"><span id="Polycyclic_Heterocycles"></span><h2 class="editable">Polycyclic Heterocycles</h2>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="clipboard_ef986b1054211c60316eea65235fbc5e7.png" style="width: 610px; height: 185px;" width="610px" height="185px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/353194/clipboard_ef986b1054211c60316eea65235fbc5e7.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=610&amp;height=185" /></p>

<p>Indole, quinoline, isoquinoline, and purine are all polycyclic aromatic heterocycles commonly found in nature. Indole, quinoline, and isoquinoline all contain a hetrocyclic ring fused to benzene. Purine is made up to two heterocyclic rings, imidazole and pyrimidine, fused together. Quinoline is found in the antimalarial drug quinine. Indole is found in the neurotransmitter serotonin. The purine ring structure is found in adenine and guanine, two important parts of DNA and in the stimulant caffeine.</p>

<center class="mt-align-center"><strong><img alt="clipboard_ee81a1543ae5795ffebe2f77fd7a91611.png" style="width: 863px; height: 184px;" width="863px" height="184px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/353300/clipboard_ee81a1543ae5795ffebe2f77fd7a91611.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=863&amp;height=184" /></strong></center>

<p>The pyridine-like nitrogen atom in quinoline and isoquinoline withdraws electrons making them both less reactive to electrophilic substitution than benzene. Likewise, quinoline (pK<sub>a</sub> = 4.9) and isoquinoline (pK<sub>a</sub> = 5.4) are less basic that a typical amine (pK<sub>a</sub> ~10-11). Quinoline and isoquinoline can both undergo electrophilic aromatic substitution but substitution on the pyridine-like ring is avoided. Quinoline usually makes a roughly equal mixture of 5 and 8 substituted products. Isoquinoline favors making the 5 substituted product with a small amount of an 8 substituted side product.</p>

<p><strong>Example</strong></p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="clipboard_e75b40a5674592af6d5b499c7f515e925.png" style="width: 410px; height: 173px;" width="410px" height="173px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/353302/clipboard_e75b40a5674592af6d5b499c7f515e925.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=410&amp;height=173" /></p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="clipboard_e40eb3355a3572ac09a5b4dd2bbe1f6cb.png" style="width: 429px; height: 147px;" width="429px" height="147px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/353303/clipboard_e40eb3355a3572ac09a5b4dd2bbe1f6cb.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=429&amp;height=147" /><strong> </strong></p>

<p>Indole has a ring nitrogen similar to to pyrrole. The lone pair electrons for this nitrogen are contained in a p orbital and are part of indole's 10 pi aromaticity. This makes indole relatively non-basic (pK<sub>a</sub> = -2) and activated toward electrophilic substitution. Indole undergo electrophilic substitution more easily than benzene and substitution typically occurs at the 3 position on the pyrrole ring.</p>

<p><strong>Example</strong></p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="clipboard_e5f9009f807233ee4bd87e826176033b7.png" style="width: 476px; height: 403px;" width="476px" height="403px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/353305/clipboard_e5f9009f807233ee4bd87e826176033b7.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=476&amp;height=403" /></p>

<p>The purine ring system contain three pyridine-like nitrogens. This lone pair electrons of theses nitrogen are contained in sp<sup>2</sup> hybrid orbitals, making them not not part of purine's 10 pi aromaticity, allowing them to retain base-like characteristics. The lone pair electrons for the remaining, pyrrole-like, nitrogen are contained in a p orbital and are part of purine's 10 pi aromaticity. This makes the remaining nitrogen relatively non-basic.</p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="clipboard_e609dd09823087c7241a09e26f4835ea0.png" style="width: 436px; height: 221px;" width="436px" height="221px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/353306/clipboard_e609dd09823087c7241a09e26f4835ea0.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=436&amp;height=221" /></p>

